  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Frost is expected in the Alleghenies tonight. Quiet \nweather conditions are anticipated thereafter.\n\nHigh pressure will be centered over the Alleghenies tonight. Winds\nare expected to go calm in locations to the west of the Blue \nRidge as the pressure gradient weakens. Locations further east \nwill likely maintain a light northerly wind through much of the \nnight. The combination of low dewpoints, clear skies, and calm \nwinds will set the stage for a near ideal radiational cooling \nnight to the west of the Blue Ridge. Most locations there will \ndrop into the 30s or lower 40s, with higher elevation valleys in\nthe Alleghenies likely dropping into the low to mid 30s. \n\nFrost Advisories continue for the Alleghenies from midnight to \n9 AM Tuesday morning. Patchy frost is possible in parts of the\ncentral Shenandoah Valley, but it is not expected to be\nwidespread enough to warrant a Frost Advisory. East of the Blue\nRidge, overnight lows are forecast to be in the 40s.\n\nQuiet weather conditions are expected locally for Tuesday and\nWednesday, with high pressure building directly overhead\ntomorrow, and then moving just offshore on Wednesday. Sunny\nskies are expected both days. Tuesday looks to be the coolest\nday of the week, with highs in the 60s for most. Tuesday night\nwill be another cool night, but with the high a bit further\neast, locations east of the Blue Ridge will have the greatest\nchance to decouple. Lows Tuesday night are forecast to be in the\nupper 30s to mid 40s. Temperatures warm a bit on Wednesday in\nsoutherly flow, with high temperatures forecast to be in the 70s\nfor most. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Remaining dry through the first half of the\nweekend before rain chances return by Sunday.\n\nThe northern latitude pattern will remain amplified during the \nmid/late week period. An embedded shortwave trough passes by to the \nnorth on Thursday which ushers in the next cold front. Like the \nearly week system, this frontal passage will also be a dry one. \nThus, the peak in warmth during the period is likely on Thursday \nwith highs reaching the upper 70s to low 80s. Another dome of \nsurface high pressure builds in for Friday and into the upcoming \nweekend. This supports a slow but steady cooling trend as highs \nreturn to the upper 60s to low 70s by Sunday. Overnight conditions \nwill be seasonable as lows drop into the mid 40s to mid 50s. At this \npoint, it does not appear any frost/freeze concerns will exist.\n\nOne area to monitor will be the southwestern Gulf of America/Bay of \nCampeche. As of 2 PM, the National Hurricane Center continues to \nraise the probabilities of development (60%) within the next 48 \nhours. Whatever does materialize and lifts toward the Gulf Coast \nfeatures a lot of forecast uncertainty. Global ensembles show an \narea of weakening low centers scattered about the Tennessee Valley \nto southeastern U.S. Depending on track and quality of tropical \nmoisture, heavier rainfall may impact some area east of the \nMississippi River. Will continue to monitor and keep precipitation \nchances around 30 to 50 percent for Sunday into next Monday.",
